WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:01.340
We're learning more tonight

00:00:01.340 --> 00:00:03.550
about a deadly car crash at speeds

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:06.110
alleged to be over 100 miles per hour.

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:08.640
A man is dead after being
hit by a car in Midtown.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:09.590
It's just tragic.

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:11.760
The crash happened on
the 10 Freeway in Fontana

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:12.890
killing four people.

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:14.630
Investigators tell us
there is a possibility

00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:16.710
that this was the result of a DUI.

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:19.080
Eye witnesses say it appeared
the driver didn't realize

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:22.030
he had struck the boy and his
mother until it was too late.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:26.380
These are only a few of
the estimated 38,800 deaths

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:28.780
on American streets in 2019.

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:30.570
And possibly even more tragically,

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:32.240
we actually have the technology

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.010
to prevent many of those deaths.

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:35.213
We're just not using it.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:41.960
This is David Zipper.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:44.440
He's a visiting fellow at
the Harvard Kennedy School

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:47.170
and first wrote about this
technology for CityLab.

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:50.570
What is the risk for
people in the United States

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:51.510
for car fatalities?

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:53.580
What has been the trend there?

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:55.000
Well, it's not been good.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.750
In the last decade in America,

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:00.270
overall road and street fatalities

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:02.220
have been relatively flat,

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:04.490
but that masks some big differences.

00:01:04.490 --> 00:01:06.610
If you're inside of an automobile,

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:07.840
it's actually gotten safer.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:10.300
Fatalities have gone
down over the last decade

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:13.770
but they've spiked for
pedestrians and for cyclists,

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:17.200
between 30% and 45% over the last decade.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:19.450
And meanwhile, if you
compare the United States

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:22.990
to other OECD countries,
other Western countries

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:25.570
especially in Europe,
we're doing much worse.

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:27.110
In Helsinki, Finland last year

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:29.540
there were literally zero deaths.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:31.890
And those risks are not spread out evenly.

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:34.370
In fact, those most likely to be killed

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:37.580
as a cyclist or a pedestrian,
they tend to be minorities,

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:40.830
they tend to be elderly,
they often are low income.

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:45.067
So, this is actually an equity
issue as much as a safety one.

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:48.640
Many city governments have worked hard

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:51.610
to address these issues,
adopting safer street initiatives

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:54.350
around improving crosswalks
and lowering speed limits.

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:56.770
But those measures can only go so far.

00:01:56.770 --> 00:02:03.160
The reality is when two tons
of glass, metal and plastic

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:07.390
hit 150 pounds of flesh and bone,

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:10.963
it's the human who's going to
lose every single time.

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:13.890
This is another David, David Friedman,

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:15.720
and he's the Vice President for Advocacy

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:17.340
at Consumer Reports.

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:19.470
You know, one of the
things we just did recently

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:22.350
was we put out a report that pointed out

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:23.440
that we could actually

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:25.810
cut the roadway fatalities in half

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:29.043
with technologies that are
already out there today.

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:32.290
Well, let's talk about speed.

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:34.750
In a big country like the United States,

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:36.420
speed sells, people like it,

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:40.200
and car companies play off
of those sorts of ideas

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:43.720
that an automobile lets you
go quickly and independently.

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:46.170
I'm reminded of an advertisement
for the Dodge Charger

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:47.870
that I saw myself

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:50.140
when I was watching the
Super Bowl a few years ago.

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:52.420
It's a wonderful thing, this game.

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:56.460
It really plays on these
ideas of being American.

00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:59.130
To bring an entire nation together.

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:00.380
And being loyal.

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:02.803
Glorious moment in time.

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:05.453
We are thankful,

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:08.150
mainly because the streets

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:10.433
are just empty as hell right now.

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:18.780
It was really striking to me.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:21.060
And I think it is very revealing

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:24.140
about how automobiles sell
this concept of speed,

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:27.163
urban speed in particular,
through their products.

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:31.840
I mean, when you sort
of look back at history,

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:34.360
the automobile really showed
up in American cities.

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:36.800
It changed the speed of the street.

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:37.910
That's Seleta Reynolds.

00:03:37.910 --> 00:03:39.270
All right, let's do it.

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:40.430
She's the General Manager

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:42.790
for the Los Angeles
Department of Transportation

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:45.180
and as she explains, lowering
the speed of vehicles

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:47.260
can dramatically shift fatality rates.

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:49.340
If you are hit by a car

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:53.270
that is traveling 20
miles an hour or slower,

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:58.760
you have a 80% to 90% chance
of surviving that crash

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:00.250
if you're on foot.

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:03.900
Once that car is going 40 miles an hour,

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:06.060
your chance of surviving that crash

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:09.773
plummets down to about 20%.

00:04:11.110 --> 00:04:13.010
Cities throughout the United States

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:15.270
have been really trying to keep

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:17.930
especially vulnerable
pedestrians and cyclists safe

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:19.420
by reducing speed limits

00:04:19.420 --> 00:04:22.710
or encouraging drivers to go slower.

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:26.120
The problem is that they can
violate that speed limit.

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:28.510
However, there is a
technological solution,

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:32.070
a simple device called a speed
governor or a speed limiter.

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:33.560
The speed governors have been around

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:35.680
for literally over a century.

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:38.550
Today, you can have a speed
governor that is pretty simple,

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:42.410
saying the vehicle can never
go above 40 miles an hour,

00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:45.480
or you can have a quote
unquote smart speed governor

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:48.690
that would adjust to the
surrounding speed limit.

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:49.870
Are there any laws in the books

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:51.380
or is there any government action

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:53.350
to sort of put these in cars?

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:55.170
Well, no.

00:04:55.170 --> 00:04:58.670
The United States, there's
really been no effort

00:04:58.670 --> 00:05:02.020
to require or even
encourage speed limiters,

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:06.210
speed governors to be
installed on personal vehicles.

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:08.888
In Europe, regulators
have been more aggressive.

00:05:08.888 --> 00:05:12.510
All new vehicles bought and
sold in the European Union

00:05:12.510 --> 00:05:15.210
from the year 2022 will have to be fitted

00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:18.680
with so-called intelligent
speed assistance technology.

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:21.310
The speed governors
there are gonna be smart

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:23.230
in that they will use data

00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:24.780
from a variety of different sources

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:28.350
to adjust based on what the
surrounding speed limit is.

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:31.600
But, and this may end up being a big but,

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:35.140
a driver will be able
to overcome the limit

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:39.150
by keeping a foot pressed
down on the accelerator.

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:42.180
That may be helpful for a
driver who wants to, say,

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:44.320
get around a truck on the highway,

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:47.210
but it does open questions
about just how much protection

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:49.970
will be afforded to
pedestrians and cyclists

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:51.830
from a truly reckless driver.

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:53.670
But there are technologies out there

00:05:53.670 --> 00:05:56.120
that can help prevent some
of the most dangerous drivers

00:05:56.120 --> 00:05:57.990
from ever even taking the wheel.

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:00.940
A woman suspected of
driving under the influence

00:06:00.940 --> 00:06:02.900
is dead this morning after driving.

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:05.820
Many roadway fatalities occur

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:11.050
within a quarter mile of a pub,
bar, drinking establishment,

00:06:11.050 --> 00:06:14.130
which means either the
driver or the pedestrian,

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:16.943
or sometimes both have been impaired.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:18.890
This is Greg Winfree,

00:06:18.890 --> 00:06:21.700
the director of the Texas
A&amp;M Transportation Institute

00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:24.650
and he points to one
maybe obvious technology,

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:25.940
the breathalyzer.

00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:30.940
Breathalyzers are typically
used to ensure compliance

00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:33.410
from folks that have been convicted

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:35.620
of a drunk driving offense.

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:37.000
You know, so there's stigma.

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:40.150
Would I want to voluntarily
put in my vehicle

00:06:40.150 --> 00:06:42.460
a device that to a passer-by

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:44.783
might say this guy is a DUI guy?

00:06:47.590 --> 00:06:49.570
The auto industry with some regulators

00:06:49.570 --> 00:06:51.810
has been developing some new technologies

00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:55.710
in a program called DADSS, or D-A-D-S-S

00:06:55.710 --> 00:06:58.770
that would actually look
at a driver's breath

00:06:58.770 --> 00:07:00.720
or their touch on the steering wheel

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:02.890
to be able to passively determine

00:07:02.890 --> 00:07:04.940
if there's alcohol in their system.

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:06.780
And then frankly they wouldn't be able

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:07.930
to operate the vehicle.

00:07:08.770 --> 00:07:11.260
That technology is still a few years away.

00:07:11.260 --> 00:07:13.530
But there's other types of
technology being developed

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:15.820
as well that have a similar goal.

00:07:15.820 --> 00:07:17.500
These technologies fall
into what are known

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:19.410
as driver monitoring systems

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:22.840
and they can detect more
than just drunk drivers.

00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:24.950
Some of them can keep an eye on your eyes

00:07:24.950 --> 00:07:26.340
and figure out whether or not

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:28.200
you've got your eyes on the road

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:30.850
and/or whether or not
you're doing proper scanning,

00:07:30.850 --> 00:07:33.470
so that it knows that
you're consciously engaged

00:07:33.470 --> 00:07:35.100
in the driving task.

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:37.370
So these sorts of driver
monitoring systems

00:07:37.370 --> 00:07:38.720
are still quite rare

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:41.590
and they're really put
only onto luxury vehicles.

00:07:41.590 --> 00:07:44.520
So the vast majority
of vehicles sold today

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:47.640
and for the foreseeable future
aren't going to have them.

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:49.150
Yeah, you know, the only exposure I've ever had

00:07:49.150 --> 00:07:52.150
to this kind of technology
is there's these Subaru ads

00:07:52.150 --> 00:07:54.470
right now, where parents
are kind of worried

00:07:54.470 --> 00:07:57.550
about their phone-addicted
teens out driving

00:07:57.550 --> 00:07:59.846
and getting into these
sort of horrific accidents.

00:07:59.846 --> 00:08:02.060
Parents have a way of imagining the worst.

00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:04.840
Automakers are trying to
differentiate themselves

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:07.713
with the quality of
their safety technology.

00:08:10.090 --> 00:08:13.220
There's another one that Honda
did a couple of years ago

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:14.663
about a guy named Mark.

00:08:14.663 --> 00:08:17.960
Mark is the best husband and
father you could ask for.

00:08:17.960 --> 00:08:20.170
Who is praised by lots of people.

00:08:20.170 --> 00:08:23.030
Great guy, best man at my wedding.

00:08:23.030 --> 00:08:24.400
Just got to keep him off the dance floor.

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:27.170
And then it shows Mark crossing a street

00:08:27.170 --> 00:08:28.750
and he's not really paying attention

00:08:28.750 --> 00:08:31.470
and he's almost hit by a Honda,

00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:34.820
but the Honda uses its
pedestrian detection system

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:38.380
to spot him and the driver stops in time.

00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:42.050
Pedestrian detection
systems are one version

00:08:42.050 --> 00:08:45.580
of, sort of like a broad
category of safety technologies

00:08:45.580 --> 00:08:49.430
known as advanced driver
assistance systems or ADAS

00:08:50.370 --> 00:08:51.833
Engaging green light.

00:08:53.069 --> 00:08:54.450
Oh my God.

00:08:54.450 --> 00:08:56.820
Automatic emergency braking.

00:08:56.820 --> 00:09:00.780
Smart cruise control or
adaptive cruise control.

00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:02.860
Lane assist.

00:09:02.860 --> 00:09:05.023
All of those are ADAS systems.

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:10.540
Those are all important safety features

00:09:10.540 --> 00:09:12.090
when you're thinking about cars

00:09:12.090 --> 00:09:15.270
operating in higher speed,
simpler environments

00:09:15.270 --> 00:09:17.350
but they are almost useless

00:09:17.350 --> 00:09:18.900
when you're talking about vehicles

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:22.096
that are operating in
complicated urban environments.

00:09:22.096 --> 00:09:25.070
AAA did a study recently showing

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:26.950
that they're highly suspect

00:09:26.950 --> 00:09:31.080
when detecting a pedestrian at night,

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:33.440
or when the vehicle is
making a right-hand turn.

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:35.440
They do a really poor job

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:38.300
even detecting adult pedestrians.

00:09:38.300 --> 00:09:42.050
Only detecting adult pedestrians
about 60% of the time

00:09:42.050 --> 00:09:45.980
and then when it comes to
smaller pedestrians and children,

00:09:45.980 --> 00:09:48.053
they hardly detected them at all.

00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:51.870
In another study, AAA also found

00:09:51.870 --> 00:09:54.220
that other ADAS technology
like lane assist

00:09:54.220 --> 00:09:57.280
and adaptive cruise control
were simply unreliable.

00:09:57.280 --> 00:09:58.950
Over 4,000 miles of driving,

00:09:58.950 --> 00:10:02.033
researchers encountered an
issue about every eight miles.

00:10:03.090 --> 00:10:04.300
And I assume this technology,

00:10:04.300 --> 00:10:05.880
along with any other technology,

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:07.510
it'll only get better over time.

00:10:07.510 --> 00:10:10.450
But I'm curious with this
technology already out there

00:10:10.450 --> 00:10:12.190
who's really pressuring
these car companies

00:10:12.190 --> 00:10:14.200
to improve it, to make it better?

00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:16.180
As of now, really no one.

00:10:16.180 --> 00:10:18.320
This is all voluntary work

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:20.320
that's being done by the automakers.

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:22.720
Some of them really do
invest a lot of money

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:26.690
and effort in it, some don't,
but it's really up to them.

00:10:26.690 --> 00:10:29.440
The shoulder belt and
lap belt are now combined

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:31.780
so that one buckle secures both belts.

00:10:31.780 --> 00:10:34.100
The reason we have seatbelts,

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:37.520
the reason we have antilock brakes

00:10:37.520 --> 00:10:41.350
is because it was mandated
by the government.

00:10:41.350 --> 00:10:43.790
Historically however,
government action around safety

00:10:43.790 --> 00:10:47.900
often faces backlash, both
by automakers and drivers.

00:10:47.900 --> 00:10:49.850
It's always been a struggle

00:10:49.850 --> 00:10:53.100
to get safety technology into automobiles.

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:56.160
It took decades to make airbags,

00:10:56.160 --> 00:10:58.890
which are commonplace today, be mandatory.

00:10:58.890 --> 00:11:03.490
In 1974, the federal
government tried to impose

00:11:03.490 --> 00:11:06.520
what's called a safety belt interlock.

00:11:06.520 --> 00:11:08.520
So you couldn't turn on your car

00:11:08.520 --> 00:11:10.610
if you weren't wearing a safety belt

00:11:10.610 --> 00:11:14.590
and within a year, auto interests
and drivers were so irate

00:11:14.590 --> 00:11:16.850
that they forced Congress to backpedal.

00:11:16.850 --> 00:11:19.730
So there's a long history
of challenges made,

00:11:19.730 --> 00:11:20.870
sometimes from drivers,

00:11:20.870 --> 00:11:23.253
oftentimes from auto companies themselves.

00:11:24.230 --> 00:11:27.020
Multiple carmakers and the
automaker lobbying group

00:11:27.020 --> 00:11:28.910
Alliance for Automotive Innovation

00:11:28.910 --> 00:11:30.803
declined to comment for this story.

00:11:31.870 --> 00:11:35.120
The car is so entangled

00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:38.846
with American ideas of independence.

00:11:38.846 --> 00:11:40.870
Built for freedom.

00:11:40.870 --> 00:11:44.313
And cars mean that to Americans.

00:11:44.888 --> 00:11:50.138
The open road, power and rebellion.

00:11:50.580 --> 00:11:53.400
Nobody can tell you how
to drive or what to do.

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:54.350
It's all about you.

00:11:55.460 --> 00:11:58.140
And that I think leads
to a real resistance

00:11:58.140 --> 00:12:01.100
among many people for
any sort of technology

00:12:01.100 --> 00:12:04.880
that it comes across as
potentially limiting their freedom,

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:07.750
even if it actually enhances substantially

00:12:07.750 --> 00:12:09.923
the safety of other people in society.

00:12:11.230 --> 00:12:15.610
You would not accept 299, 737's

00:12:15.610 --> 00:12:17.600
which is crashing on an annual basis.

00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:21.640
One plane crash, crashing with
that level of souls on it

00:12:21.640 --> 00:12:23.180
is not lost on society,

00:12:23.180 --> 00:12:26.870
but for some reason in
the US, we don't focus,

00:12:26.870 --> 00:12:31.410
don't pay attention to
38,000 road fatalities,

00:12:31.410 --> 00:12:34.773
that it's not the human
cry that it should be.

00:12:36.510 --> 00:12:38.020
We're two things in America.

00:12:38.020 --> 00:12:39.170
We're a car culture.

00:12:39.170 --> 00:12:41.810
We love our cars and we love our tech.

00:12:41.810 --> 00:12:44.090
People love technology at
the end of the day, right?

00:12:44.090 --> 00:12:46.210
So, the challenge isn't the engineering.

00:12:46.210 --> 00:12:48.200
The challenge is the political will

00:12:48.200 --> 00:12:51.280
of getting these technologies on every car

00:12:51.280 --> 00:12:53.040
so that we could take on

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:56.550
the nearly 40,000 lives lost every year

00:12:56.550 --> 00:12:58.055
and cut that about in half.

